{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Spec Creation Validation",
  "description": "Triggers web search validation when new spec files are created to ensure initial designs and requirements are research-backed",
  "version": "2",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileCreated",
    "patterns": [
      "**/*requirements.md",
      "**/*design.md",
      "**/*tasks.md",
      "**/*bugfix.md"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "A spec file has been created. You MUST use Kiro's built-in web search tool to validate the content. Do the following:\n\n1. RESEARCH VALIDATION: Use web search to verify that any AWS services, architectural patterns, libraries, or technical approaches mentioned in this file align with current best practices and official documentation.\n\n2. WELL-ARCHITECTED CHECK: For any AWS architecture decisions, search for and cross-reference against the AWS Well-Architected Framework six pillars (Operational Excellence, Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization, Sustainability).\n\n3. ANTI-PATTERN DETECTION: Search for known anti-patterns, gotchas, or limitations related to the services and approaches described in the spec.\n\n4. DEPENDENCY VALIDATION: For any libraries or frameworks mentioned, verify they are actively maintained, have no critical vulnerabilities, and are the recommended choice for the use case.\n\n5. TECHNICAL DEBT CHECK: Review the content for any shortcuts, workarounds, or deferred improvements. If found, log them to the project's open-tech-debt.md file following the format defined in the technical-debt-management steering doc.\n\n6. REPORT: Provide a brief summary of findings. Flag any concerns, suggest improvements if warranted, and cite source URLs for key validations.\n\nDo NOT skip the web search step. Every validation must be backed by a search result."
  }
}